<section class="bg-base-100 text-base-content">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[90rem] space-y-24 px-4 py-20 md:space-y-36 md:px-10 md:py-32">
    <article class="grid items-center gap-10 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-12">
      <div class="aspect-[4/5] md:col-span-6" data-drift data-grade><img src="/media/window.jpg" class="size-full object-cover"></div>
      <div class="md:col-span-5 md:col-start-8" data-reveal>
        <p class="tag text-primary">I · The warehouse</p>
        <h2 class="mt-6 text-5xl text-balance md:text-6xl">Rope, sailcloth and lamp oil</h2>
        <p class="prose-ember mt-6">Chandler Lane was named for what it sold. Our building went up in 1868 to hold the ropes, the sailcloth and the lamp oil that schooners took on before leaving Portland, and its bricks still smell faintly of tar on a wet night. When we found it in 2020 it had been a printer's, a garage and, for twelve years, nothing at all.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <figure class="mx-auto max-w-4xl border-y border-base-300 py-14 text-center md:py-20" data-reveal>
      <blockquote><p class="display text-4xl leading-tight text-balance md:text-6xl">“We wanted a room where the fire is the only clock.”</p></blockquote>
      <figcaption class="tag mt-8 text-base-content/70">Mara Lindqvist, chef</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <article class="grid items-center gap-10 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-12">
      <div class="aspect-[4/5] md:order-2 md:col-span-6 md:col-start-7" data-drift data-grade><img src="/media/hearth.jpg" class="size-full object-cover"></div>
      <div class="md:order-1 md:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="tag text-primary">II · The hearth</p>
        <h2 class="mt-6 text-5xl text-balance md:text-6xl">A fire that is lit at four</h2>
        <p class="prose-ember mt-6">Mara built the hearth with a mason from Waldoboro where the loading door used to be: an open grate of iron, two feet off the floor, burning maple for heat and oak for smoke. There is no gas line in the kitchen. Everything we cook passes over it, close to the flames or buried in the embers.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="grid items-center gap-10 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-12">
      <div class="aspect-[4/5] md:col-span-6" data-drift data-grade><img src="/media/brick-rack.jpg" class="size-full object-cover"></div>
      <div class="md:col-span-5 md:col-start-8" data-reveal>
        <p class="tag text-primary">III · The cellar</p>
        <h2 class="mt-6 text-5xl text-balance md:text-6xl">Bottles under the vaults</h2>
        <p class="prose-ember mt-6">Below the dining room, the warehouse's brick vaults keep a steady fifty-five degrees all year. Julien filled them slowly, a few cases at a time from growers he had visited, until there were three hundred bottles and a long table in the middle that became the Cellar Room.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
